#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa3dc44f-86e9-58c8-96da-3f517ff52007" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2281b5d8-2a29-52ff-ac92-7b3ac7d5b0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344238} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8ce24f-1c15-5c95-bc11-61cc63059ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344239} {:answer/id #uuid "09a0fb5a-e2f9-5dcb-9aed-ee906167c0a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344240} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8c718c-8048-5446-a15f-91f36a7ba1f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344241} {:answer/id #uuid "fc79813d-2bfd-5742-b427-85fd7cbf7d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc8c718c-8048-5446-a15f-91f36a7ba1f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344241} :answer-field/id #uuid "02a72223-5b93-5038-9629-5363cfa57566" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05d837ac-69f8-5c45-8fb6-a9ac4817dd42" :question/math-academy-id "q-161496" :question/problem "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nis defined over the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}y\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-y}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "A type I region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {u}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/assets/1bd4b6d0b1aa955921c1f7855d3fbd95417072a67504d9de9be44dfbbed592e5.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type I region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,y)=0,\\,{u}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}y\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dx\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1+4=5.\n$$"}]}
